  "rulings": [
    {"oracle_id":"14f55fc9-ec19-4b4a-b3de-d75898c19edf","published_at":"2023-06-16","source":"wotc","comment":"If there are three or more ribbon counters on Prize Pig after you put ribbon counters on it, all of the counters will be removed. It does not matter whether or not Prize Pig is already untapped."},
    {
      "comment": "You choose a target when the ability triggers. You don’t choose whether to pay {1}{W} until the ability resolves.",
      "oracle_id": "956250da-532a-4457-8696-73915be56943",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Cradle of Vitality triggers just once for each life-gaining event, whether it’s 1 life from Deathgreeter or 8 life from Soul’s Grace. You pay the cost just once, but the targeted creature will get a number of counters equal to the amount of life you gained.",
      "oracle_id": "956250da-532a-4457-8696-73915be56943",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If multiple creatures you control with lifelink deal damage simultaneously, each instance is a single life-gain event and the ability will trigger separately for each of them.",
      "oracle_id": "956250da-532a-4457-8696-73915be56943",
      "published_at": "2014-02-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In some unusual cases, you can gain life even though your life total actually decreases. For example, if you are being attacked by two 3/3 creatures and you block one with a 2/2 creature with lifelink, your life total will decrease by 1 even though you’ve gained 2 life. You’ll put two +1/+1 counters on the enchanted creature.",
      "oracle_id": "163ece8a-0479-4bb2-b559-581985d1898b",
      "published_at": "2014-02-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In a Two-Headed Giant game, life gained by your teammate won’t cause the ability to trigger, even though it causes your team’s life total to increase.",
      "oracle_id": "163ece8a-0479-4bb2-b559-581985d1898b",
      "published_at": "2014-02-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If you gain an amount of life “for each” of something, that life is gained as one event and the ability triggers only once.",
      "oracle_id": "64c54c9f-0550-41e2-a44a-5892a199f670",
      "published_at": "2020-06-23",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In a Two-Headed Giant game, life gained by your teammate won't cause the ability to trigger, even though it caused your team's life total to increase.",
      "oracle_id": "64c54c9f-0550-41e2-a44a-5892a199f670",
      "published_at": "2020-06-23",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If enchanted creature is dealt lethal damage at the same time that you gain life, it won't receive a counter from its ability in time to save it.",
      "oracle_id": "64c54c9f-0550-41e2-a44a-5892a199f670",
      "published_at": "2020-06-23",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Each creature with lifelink dealing combat damage causes a separate life-gaining event. For example, if two creatures you control with lifelink deal combat damage at the same time, the ability will trigger twice. However, if a single creature you control with lifelink deals combat damage to multiple creatures, players, and/or planeswalkers at the same time (perhaps because it has trample or was blocked by more than one creature), the ability will trigger only once.",
      "oracle_id": "64c54c9f-0550-41e2-a44a-5892a199f670",
      "published_at": "2020-06-23",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a creature you control would enter the battlefield with a number of +1/+1 counters on it, it enters with that many plus one instead.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f3da21-af6d-450e-bf0b-985d158418e6",
      "published_at": "2023-09-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If two or more effects attempt to modify how many counters would be put on a creature you control, you choose the order to apply those effects, no matter who controls the sources of those effects.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f3da21-af6d-450e-bf0b-985d158418e6",
      "published_at": "2023-09-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Each additional Hardened Scales you control will increase the number of +1/+1 counters placed on a creature you control by one.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f3da21-af6d-450e-bf0b-985d158418e6",
      "published_at": "2023-09-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Abilities that Slivers grant, as well as power/toughness boosts, are cumulative. However, for some abilities, like indestructible and the ability granted by Belligerent Sliver, having more than one instance of the ability doesn’t provide any additional benefit.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If you change the creature type of a Sliver you control so it’s no longer a Sliver, it will no longer be affected by its own ability. Its ability will continue to affect other Sliver creatures you control.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Slivers in this set affect only Sliver creatures you control. They don’t grant bonuses to your opponents’ Slivers. This is the same way Slivers from the Magic 2014 core set worked, while Slivers in earlier sets granted bonuses to all Slivers.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Damage dealt to a Sliver with indestructible is still marked on that creature. If it has lethal damage marked on it and it loses indestructible (perhaps because Sliver Hivelord leaves the battlefield), it will be destroyed.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "It can target delayed triggered abilities. For example, a card that says \"Whenever a player cycles a card, you may exile target creature. If you do, return that creature to the battlefield at the beginning of the end step.\" triggers when a player cycles a card and creates a delayed trigger that happens at the beginning of the end step. You can choose to target the \"at the beginning of the end step\" trigger when it is placed on the stack at end of turn.",
      "oracle_id": "b3b00911-ece7-4484-bc36-f211ce72b6cc",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "An activated ability has a \"Cost: Effect\" format. Look for the colon. A triggered ability starts with \"when\", \"whenever\", or \"at\".",
      "oracle_id": "b3b00911-ece7-4484-bc36-f211ce72b6cc",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Turn-based actions and special actions like the normal card draw, combat damage, or turning a face-down creature face up can't be targeted.",
      "oracle_id": "b3b00911-ece7-4484-bc36-f211ce72b6cc",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "This is not modal. If the target changes from an artifact to an enchantment or vice versa, this still destroys it.",
      "oracle_id": "a7e97fa9-4b72-4548-b854-5be5f18a6f1a",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The controller of Vedalken Orrery can cast nonland artifact, creature, enchantment, and sorcery cards any time they could cast an instant. This includes on other players' turns and during the upkeep step, draw step, combat phase, and end step.",
      "oracle_id": "60d640a4-032b-4279-ac75-8f200ca776fb",
      "published_at": "2004-12-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "This applies only to casting spells. It does not, for example, change when you may activate abilities that can only be activated \"any time you could cast a sorcery\".",
      "oracle_id": "60d640a4-032b-4279-ac75-8f200ca776fb",
      "published_at": "2010-06-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Effects that would replace gaining life with another effect won’t apply because it’s impossible for players to gain life.",
      "oracle_id": "af0dd100-67f0-4d7c-a65b-b2c7149288f7",
      "published_at": "2012-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Spells and abilities that would cause a player to gain life still resolve, but the life-gain part has no effect.",
      "oracle_id": "af0dd100-67f0-4d7c-a65b-b2c7149288f7",
      "published_at": "2012-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an effect says to set a player’s life total to a certain number and that number is higher than the player’s current life total, that part of the effect won’t do anything. (If the number is lower than the player’s current life total, the effect will work as normal.)",
      "oracle_id": "af0dd100-67f0-4d7c-a65b-b2c7149288f7",
      "published_at": "2012-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The amount of life lost is calculated when the triggered ability resolves. For example, if a player has 20 life and there are two Havoc Festivals on the battlefield, the first ability will cause that player to lose 10 life and then the second ability will cause that player to lose 5 life.",
      "oracle_id": "af0dd100-67f0-4d7c-a65b-b2c7149288f7",
      "published_at": "2012-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In a Two-Headed Giant game, the last ability triggers for each player. Any life loss is applied to the team’s life total. For example, if the team has 30 life, the first ability will cause the player (and thus the team) to lose 15 life and then the second ability will cause the other player (and thus the team) to lose 8 life. The team will end up having 7 life.",
      "oracle_id": "af0dd100-67f0-4d7c-a65b-b2c7149288f7",
      "published_at": "2012-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an effect would set your life total to a specific number that's higher than your current life total, that effect would cause you to gain life equal to the difference. Boon Reflection will then double the amount of life that effect would cause you to gain. For example, if you have 3 life and an effect says that your life total “becomes 10,” your life total will actually become 17.",
      "oracle_id": "b86d3142-568a-4eea-b1ae-5fcfc1453533",
      "published_at": "2020-08-07",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In a Two-Headed Giant game, only Boon Reflection's controller is affected by it. If that player's teammate gains life, Boon Reflection will have no effect, even when that life gain is applied to the shared team life total.",
      "oracle_id": "b86d3142-568a-4eea-b1ae-5fcfc1453533",
      "published_at": "2020-08-07",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The effects of multiple Boon Reflections are cumulative. For example, if you control two Boon Reflections, you'll gain four times the original amount of life. If you control three Boon Reflections, you'll gain eight times the original amount, and so on.",
      "oracle_id": "b86d3142-568a-4eea-b1ae-5fcfc1453533",
      "published_at": "2020-08-07",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "oracle_id": "a5350327-e84c-44b6-8359-87882e521048",
      "published_at": "2026-08-21",
      "source": "wotc",
      "comment": "If more than one creature you control with lifelink deals combat damage at the same time, Niv-Mizzet's triggered ability will trigger once for each of those creatures."
    }
  ],
